# Qt-facing overrides. Each check here has a concrete reason its advice cannot
# be taken -- mostly about Qt predating the guidelines, never about the warnings
# being tiresome. Everything that *could* be fixed was fixed first: the missing
# <QWidget>, <QObject>, <QSize>, <QPaintEvent> and "worker.hpp" includes, and a
# genuine bugprone-integer-division in needle.cpp that only a linter would spot.
#
# readability-identifier-naming: the root config asks for lower_case functions.
# Qt names its virtuals in camelBack -- paintEvent, sizeHint -- and an override
# has to match the base exactly or it is not an override. The check cannot tell
# an obligation from a convention violation.
#
# cppcoreguidelines-owning-memory: `new QPushButton(this)` looks like a leak and
# is not. Passing a parent hands ownership to that parent, whose destructor
# deletes the whole subtree -- QObject parenting *is* RAII, just not a flavour
# gsl::owner<> can express. Replacing it with unique_ptr would mean two owners
# for one object, which is how a double free happens. The rule here instead:
# every `new` in this directory passes a parent, and none of them is ever
# deleted by hand. Anything that breaks that rule is a real finding.
#
# cppcoreguidelines-prefer-member-initializer wants five of the eight widgets
# hoisted into the initialiser list -- exactly the five whose `new` takes only a
# parent. It cannot suggest the other three, which also pass a label. Taking
# half the advice would split one readable construction sequence across two
# places for no gain, since `new Widget(this)` leaves nothing uninitialised that
# an initialiser list would have protected.
#
# misc-include-cleaner and readability-redundant-access-specifiers: see
# libs/av_qt/.clang-tidy, which explains both at length.
#
# misc-const-correctness wants `const QApplication app`, because main() never
# writes to it. Qt does, constantly -- through the global qApp pointer its
# constructor installed. Declaring an object const and then modifying it is
# undefined behaviour, so this is one of the cases where the check names a bug
# rather than a cleanup. libs/av_qt/tests/.clang-tidy hits the same wall.

InheritParentConfig: true

Checks: >
  -readability-identifier-naming,
  -cppcoreguidelines-owning-memory,
  -cppcoreguidelines-prefer-member-initializer,
  -misc-include-cleaner,
  -readability-redundant-access-specifiers,
  -misc-const-correctness
